  "modern_form": "Pokorny's melǵ- (mela-ǵ-?) 'abstreifen, wischen', in the European languages 'melken'; modern handbooks split it into PIE *h2melǵ- 'to milk' and *h2merǵ- 'to wipe, strip off'",
  "note": "The root box reproduces Pokorny p.722 word for word: 'melǵ- (oder mela-ǵ-?) ‚abstreifen, wischen‘, europ. ‚melken‘; Präs. melǵ-mi, Pl. ml̥ǵ-mes, Partiz. Perf. Pass. ml̥ǵ-tó-; molǵo- ‚Melken‘' — so language label, gloss ('to wipe off, to milk') and page are all exactly right, and Watkins repeats it ('melg-. To rub off; also to milk ... [Pok. melg- 722]'). Mann (IEC) even explains the semantic bridge: 'The semantic association of ‚wipe‘ and ‚milk‘ is apparent in Latvian slauku, slaucit ‚wipe‘; slaucu, slaukt ‚milk‘'. What has changed since 1959 is the unity of the root: LIV, Cheung and Wiktionary keep *h2merǵ- 'to pluck, wipe' (Gk. amérgō, Skt. mṛj-, Ir. *Hmarz) apart from *h2melǵ- 'to milk' (Lat. mulgeō, Gmc. *melkan), the r/l difference being original rather than dissimilatory; Pokorny himself flagged the doubt on the same page ('diese ar. Worte können auch *merǵ- enthalten'). So the two Avestan branches of this chart hang on the older, unified reconstruction — see nodes #1 and #3.",
